[Music]  [Music]  the legitimacy of governments  is a crucial factor not just for its own  sake but in terms of their effectiveness  [Music]  why is it that those answers are right  that you select people that they get all  this  evidence they're given time to reason  and they come up with a better answer  but that's in your opinion  [Music]  ordinary people and we're all ordinary  people that's not distinguishing one  group from the rest  are very good at coming to decisions  when they do that reasoning  collectively  we're talking about democracy and from  trump to putin from  bolsonaro to assad from the far right to  the far left  everyone claims to be in favor of  democracy but in what form  and in whose interest in the west  democracy has meant electing others to  decide for us  and now many people argue that democracy  is in trouble  were we mistaken to imagine that  democracy is necessarily  progressive and delivers for the people  can we harness  new technologies like blockchain to  enable a new  more direct form of democracy that gives  everyone a voice  or is the very idea of giving everyone a  voice an illusion that leaves us victim  to those with power and influence  democracy is supposed to give power to  the people  but how can this really be achieved on  all very big questions but luckily we've  got a great panel  to help us answer them so julian pagini  is the author of lots of best-selling  books on philosophy  including how the world thinks and is  the co-founder of the philosopher's  magazine  claire foxx is the founding director of  the academy of ideas  a former brexit mep and panelist on  bbc radio 4 show the moral maze and  matthew taylor  is a former senior advisor to tony blair  and he's currently chief executive of  the royal society for the encouragement  of arts manufacturers and commerce  otherwise known as the rsa so we're  going to start by asking each of the  three panelists to do just a three  minute pitch  to summarize their ideas on this subject  on the question  is more democracy always the answer and  i'm going to start with you matty taylor  well the first thing i want to say is  that this is an important  subject in that um the legitimacy of  governments  uh is a crucial factor not just for its  own sake but in terms of their  effectiveness  and we've seen that in this covid crisis  that it's been  those governments which uh have greater  legitimacy and greater trust  that have been able to respond more  effectively than governments that lack  that legitimacy because they've  alienated large parts of the population  or they're corrupt or incompetent or  some combination  of the all of them and if legitimacy has  been important to the way in which we  have dealt with this  crisis it'll be even more important in  relation to  the much more serious existential crisis  of the climate emergency so i'm glad  we're talking about this it's an  important  question is more democracy always the  answer  well no not more democracy but better  democracy  um and i want to make one argument in  this  initial part of the debate and that is  the representative democracy which is  the system that marianne described to  you the system where we  uh elect every few years a small number  of people  to make decisions on our behalf between  elections is simply  inadequate uh it's inadequate for a  number of different  uh reasons it's an adequate way the  parties have become hollowed out and  internally polarized  um it's inadequate because we as  citizens have become  more demanding and our expectations have  risen but it's particularly  because it's just a very blunt mandate  we probably prefer supermarkets to  parliament but imagine how popular  supermarkets would be if every five  years we voted for our favorite  supermarket  and the one that won with about 30  percent of people voting for it was then  when we were forced to go to  and what is more that supermarket could  choose what to put in our  basket and how much we had to pay for it  we would probably hate supermarkets but  that's the kind of level of choice we  have when it comes to our democratic  system  there is however thankfully a way of  enhancing representation not get rid of  it because ultimately in a large complex  society you have to have representative  democracy  as the sovereign body but you can  enhance it by the use of deliberative  democracy  which is a model much more like the  athenian original  it's a model where a representative  group of citizens  independently selected to represent a  different opinions  different demography work together  over a period of time maybe one two  three weekends  listen to expert views they have  specialist  mediators there who don't have a view on  the issue but are there to make it work  and at the end of it they generate  recommendations which they pass on to  representatives  and what we know from all around the  world is that this works  that the citizens who undertake it enjoy  it often when they  start they say i can't do this i have no  expertise at the end they've loved it  they want to do it more often  and that they generate incredibly  sensible and thoughtful conclusions  and the one example i give you and i  could give you a hundred is that in  ireland a couple of years ago when they  had the very contentious issue of  constitutional reform which involved  basically liberalizing their abortion  laws it could have totally divided the  country had it simply been  decisions made by the members of the  irish parliament instead they had a  citizens assembly over several weekends  and that citizens assembly designed the  process which ultimately led to a  referendum  um so you had their deliberative  democracy informing the representatives  who then passed legislation to create a  a referendum uh it was overwhelmingly in  favor of liberalization and that issue  rather than dividing ireland if anything  it brought ireland  together but we don't need more  democracy we need better democracy and  we have the tools to do that by  enhancing representation  with deliberation thanks claire  let's hear your pitch okay i think that  is a crisis  in western democracy because many have  turned against the idea  that citizens equal citizens can be  trusted to make decisions actually we  think  uh matthew reflected some of that i  think it was  brought ahead in the uk  in the eu referendum because it was  i thought it was interesting uh marianne  when you introduce this and the blurb  for the session when it says  democracy is electing others to decide  for us but  actually we elect others to enact and  represent  what we have decided they don't decide  for us it's a different way round  and i think parliament actually refused  to enact what  uh the masses decided in 2016 and that  brings us to a crisis  you know that popular determination to  leave the eu  took elected politicians by surprise  they'd assumed  that the agreement of the front penches  of the two main parties  the bbc itb channel 4 the big business  and the banks  would be enough to ensure the electorate  did what was expected  and when they didn't i think there's  been a backlash against the electorate  and if you think about how the  referendum result was explained it was  somehow that the  vote had been achieved by cheating or  manipulation  uh that the the idea was that the  electorate were  uh incompetent they were not able to  make the right decision because they'd  been  ill-informed people even talked about  their poor education  and i think that and and they were also  accused of being populist i mean that  became a word that's used as an  insult to uh people who support  democracy these days  and that somehow they kind of only voted  the way they did because they were  influenced by  you know some maverick big authoritarian  figure  so i think once you decide or believe  that the masses are unfit to influence  the direction of society or  lacking in capacity not trustworthy in  that way  then i think that representative  democracy does start to feel hollowed  out but it's  partly had already been hollowed out  it had become slightly uh caveated and  hemmed in  previous to that by the emergence of  unelected judiciary and courts and  transnational  institutions that had tried to minimize  the influence of the people on  opinion and we know that the sense is  that  we know best the kind of paternalism  that will protect you  will take away the difficult choices and  everything's too complicated for you  obviously undermines an electorate and  their autonomy  and i do think that threatens uh  democracy  i think in other words a technocratic  rule can threaten democracy  rather than any kind of principle it  relies on  evidence and experts to justify  action and decisions are ring fenced  away from accountability  um and i think that in some ways it  means that uh  politicians uh our politicians uh  use uh evidence and science and and  issues like that to justify  making difficult decisions taking  difficult decisions away from the  democratic arena  and in some ways the kovid crisis raises  all of these things as we all sit on the  couch and watch  everybody else decide what happens on  our behalf  okay thanks so julian is is more  democracy always the answer  yeah i mean like matthew it's not more  it's better i think there are the two  currents here which you want to avoid  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

